1) useMemo()
Why useMemo is needed

Normally, when a component re-renders:

All calculations inside component execute again.

Sometimes calculations are expensive:

Sorting huge lists
Filtering large data
Complex computations

useMemo() avoids recalculating unnecessarily.

Example Without useMemo =>
function App() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  const expensiveValue = heavyCalculation();

  return (
    <>
      <h1>{count}</h1>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>
        Increment
      </button>
    </>
  );
}

Problem:
a) heavyCalculation() runs every time component renders
b) Even if calculation result never changes


Example With useMemo =>
import { useMemo, useState } from "react";

function App() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  const expensiveValue = useMemo(() => {
    return heavyCalculation();
  }, []);

  return (
    <>
      <h1>{count}</h1>

      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>
        Increment
      </button>
    </>
  );
}

Now:
a) heavyCalculation() runs only once
b) Re-renders won’t recalculate it

Dependency Example =>
const filteredUsers = useMemo(() => {
   return users.filter(user => user.active);
}, [users]);

Meaning:
Recalculate only when users changes

Real-Time Use Cases

Use useMemo() for:
1) Filtering large lists
2) Sorting data
3) Expensive computations
4) Derived state calculations
5) Preventing unnecessary child renders

Simple Interview Definition

“useMemo is a React Hook used to cache the result of expensive computations and recompute them only when dependencies change, helping to improve rendering performance.”


2) React.memo()

a) Why React.memo is needed

Normally in React:
When parent component re-renders,
All child components also re-render by default.

Even if child props are unchanged.
This can affect performance in large applications.

React.memo() solves this problem.


b) Example Without React.memo =>

import React, { useState } from "react";

function Child() {
   console.log("Child Rendered");

   return <h1>Child Component</h1>;
}

function Parent() {

   const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

   return (
      <>
         <h1>{count}</h1>

         <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>
            Increment
         </button>

         <Child />
      </>
   );
}

What happens?

Whenever button clicked:
Parent re-renders
Child also re-renders
Even though Child data never changed.

Example With React.memo
import React, { useState } from "react";

const Child = React.memo(() => {

   console.log("Child Rendered");

   return <h1>Child Component</h1>;
});

function Parent() {

   const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

   return (
      <>
         <h1>{count}</h1>

         <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>
            Increment
         </button>

         <Child />
      </>
   );
}

Now What Happens?

When button clicked:
Parent re-renders
Child does NOT re-render

Because:
Props are same
React.memo caches previous render

Important Point

React.memo() works by comparing props between renders.
By default, it does a shallow comparison of props.

Meaning:
Primitive values compare by value
Objects/functions compare by reference

Real-Time Use Cases

Use React.memo for:
a) Large UI components
b) Tables
c) Charts
d) Frequently re-rendering parents
e) Expensive child rendering

React.memo() only works for: Functional components
Equivalent for class component: PureComponent

Simple Interview Definition
“React.memo is a Higher Order Component used to prevent unnecessary re-rendering of functional components by memoizing the rendered output when props remain unchanged.”


React.memo is a specialized HOC provided by React for performance optimization.
const MemoizedComponent = React.memo(MyComponent);
What enhancement does React.memo add?
It adds:
memoization
prevents unnecessary re-renders
So the returned component only re-renders when props change.


Main Difference
| Feature  | React.memo()         | useMemo()             |
| -------- | -------------------- | --------------------- |
| Used for | Component            | Value / Calculation   |
| Prevents | Component re-render  | Recalculation         |
| Works on | Functional component | Expensive computation |
| Returns  | Memoized component   | Memoized value        |


3) usecallback()

Why useCallback is needed

In React:
const handleClick = () => {
   console.log("clicked");
}
This function gets recreated every time component re-renders.

Normally this is fine, but it becomes a problem when:
Function is passed to child components
Child components use React.memo
Frequent re-renders happen

Problem Without useCallback =>

import React, { useState } from "react";

const Child = React.memo(({ onClick }) => {
  console.log("Child Rendered");

  return <button onClick={onClick}>Click</button>;
});

function App() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  const handleClick = () => {
    console.log("Button clicked");
  };

  return (
    <>
      <h1>{count}</h1>

      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>
        Increment
      </button>

      <Child onClick={handleClick} />
    </>
  );
}
What happens here?

When count changes:
a) Parent component re-renders
b) handleClick function recreated
c) Child gets new function reference
d) Child re-renders unnecessarily

Even though logic is same.

Solution Using useCallback =>

import React, { useState, useCallback } from "react";

const Child = React.memo(({ onClick }) => {
  console.log("Child Rendered");

  return <button onClick={onClick}>Click</button>;
});

function App() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  const handleClick = useCallback(() => {
    console.log("Button clicked");
  }, []);

  return (
    <>
      <h1>{count}</h1>

      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>
        Increment
      </button>

      <Child onClick={handleClick} />
    </>
  );
}

Now What Happens?

When count changes:

a) Parent re-renders
b) handleClick reference stays same
c) Child does NOT re-render unnecessarily


Syntax
const memoizedFunction = useCallback(() => {
   // logic
}, [dependencies]);

Function recreated only when dependencies change.


Real-Time Use Cases

Use useCallback() when:

Passing functions to child components
Using React.memo
Preventing unnecessary renders
Optimizing large applications

Important Interview Point =>

useCallback does NOT stop component re-rendering by itself.
It only: Keeps same function reference between renders.
Usually combined with: React.memo

Difference useMemo & useCallback

useMemo => Memoizes a value

const sortedList = useMemo(() => {
  return items.sort();
}, [items]);

Here: sortedList is a memoized VALUE

useCallback => Memoizes a function

const handleClick = useCallback(() => {
  console.log("clicked");
}, []);

Here: handleClick is a memoized FUNCTION

Easy Way to Remember
useMemo     -> memoized result/value
useCallback -> memoized function


4) REACT APP PERFORMANCE OPTIMIZATION TECHIQUES

Performance improves.
In a React application, optimization mainly focuses on:

Faster rendering
Reduced unnecessary re-renders
Smaller bundle size
Better API handling
Improved user experience

Here are the important React app optimizations commonly discussed in interviews:

1. Component Re-render Optimization
Use React.memo()

Prevents unnecessary re-rendering of functional components when props don’t change.

const User = React.memo(({name}) => {
  return <h1>{name}</h1>
})

Use when:
Component renders frequently
Props rarely change

Use useMemo()

Memoizes expensive calculations.

const sortedList = useMemo(() => {
   return users.sort();
}, [users]);

Prevents recalculating on every render.

Use useCallback()

Memoizes functions.

const handleClick = useCallback(() => {
   fetchData();
}, []);

Useful when passing functions to child components.


2. Code Splitting / Lazy Loading

Instead of loading the entire app initially, load components only when needed.

const Dashboard = React.lazy(() => import('./Dashboard'));
<Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>
   <Dashboard />
</Suspense>

Benefits:

Faster initial page load
Reduced bundle size

3. Bundle Size Optimization
Remove unused libraries
Avoid heavy libraries if lighter alternatives exist.

Example:
Use native JS methods instead of large utility libraries when possible.


4. State Management Optimization
Avoid unnecessary global state
Keep state local whenever possible.

Bad: Storing every UI field in Redux/Context
Good: Use component state for local UI data


5) Caching

Use libraries like:
React Query
SWR

Benefits:
API caching
Background refresh
Reduced network calls

6) Pagination Instead of Large Data Loading

Instead of loading 10,000 records:
Use pagination
Infinite scrolling

Improves:
Rendering performance
API response time

7) Use CDN & Browser Caching
Static assets caching
CDN for images/scripts

Improves loading speed globally.

8) 10. Production Build Optimization

Use: npm run build

Production build:
Minifies JS
Removes unused code
Improves performance